  "doc.title": "Why Compute Waits for Memory · an interactive long essay",
  "doc.desc": "A processor can execute quadrillions of operations per second, so why does it spend most of its time waiting for memory? Ten interactive models covering the 5-stage pipeline, cache simulators, matrix tiling, the memory wall, Little's Law, the roofline model, LLM inference, HBM4, and the energy frontier.",
  "nav.back": "← Knowledge Index",
  "nav.lang": "Language",
  "toc.label": "Contents",
  "toc.title": "Contents",

  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems & Architecture · an interactive long essay",
  "hero.title": "Why Compute Waits for Memory",
  "hero.sub": "Processors can run quadrillions of operations per second.<br>Why do AI chips and your computer spend most of their time idling?",
  "hero.lead": "Pick up the spec sheet of any modern AI chip: nearly a quadrillion floating-point operations per second. It sounds impossibly fast. Yet when you actually run programs—especially when asking a large language model to produce text token by token—the arithmetic units often sit idle more than 90% of the time. This essay asks one question: <strong>if raw compute is already unimaginably fast, why does compute spend its life waiting for memory?</strong> Below are ten interactive models you can drive yourself. By the end, you will see that the answer boils down to three things: <strong>physical distance</strong> (fetching a number is hundreds of times slower and costs hundreds of times more energy than computing with it), <strong>the widening gap</strong> (compute speed has grown vastly faster than memory bandwidth), and <strong>reuse</strong> (from CPU caches to batching, every major system optimization is fundamentally about moving data as few times as possible).",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. Each model implements a standard computer architecture equation or a small-scale simulator (pipeline, set-associative cache, roofline, Little's Law, etc.) rather than canned animations. Real chips also feature out-of-order execution, hardware prefetchers, and compiler scheduling; each chapter concludes with explicit notes on the simplifications made. All hardware specifications and frontier figures cite their sources and verification dates (as of November 2026).",

  /* ───── 01 ───── */
  "ch1.title": "Bubbles in the Pipeline",
  "ch1.law": "Modern processors break each instruction into five staggered stages like a factory assembly line; but if just one instruction stalls waiting for memory, every instruction behind it gets stuck, and the arithmetic units are forced to swallow bubbles.",
  "ch1.p1": "In <a href=\"../switches-that-compute/#ch9\">How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic</a>, a computer fetches instructions and computes numbers tick by tick against a clock. If hardware waited for each instruction to finish completely before starting the next, most execution units would sit idle. A classic RISC processor splits each instruction across a five-stage pipeline: <strong>Instruction Fetch (IF) → Instruction Decode (ID) → Execute (EX) → Memory Access (MEM) → Write-back (WB)</strong>. Ideally, once the pipeline fills, one instruction retires every single clock cycle, giving an average throughput of 1 cycle per instruction.",
  "ch1.p2": "Real-world programs, however, run into two obstacles: an instruction often needs a value that was just computed or fetched by the preceding instruction (data dependency), and if the target value is not already in the L1 cache right next to the core, the MEM stage stretches from 1 cycle to dozens or even hundreds of cycles. In the 6-instruction loop below, the first instruction <code>LD</code> loads into register <code>R1</code>, and the very next instruction <code>ADD</code> immediately consumes <code>R1</code>.",
  "ch1.model.title": "5-Stage Pipeline and Memory Stall Simulator",
  "ch1.model.how": "Toggle the memory latency (from 1 cycle on an L1 hit to 200 cycles on a DRAM miss) and the “Data Forwarding” switch, then step or play. Red boxes are pipeline stall bubbles caused by waiting on memory; gold boxes are bubbles waiting on arithmetic results.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "5-stage pipeline instruction timing chart showing instruction states across IF, ID, EX, MEM, and WB stages with stall bubbles",
