  "ch1.cv.memstall": "Wait MEM",
  "ch1.say.l1f": "L1 hit with forwarding enabled: 6 instructions take {cyc} cycles (CPI {cpi}), with only 1 load-use interlock bubble, resulting in {wait}% stall time.",
  "ch1.say.nofwd": "With forwarding disabled, subsequent instructions must wait until the previous one completes WB to write back to the register: total time rises to {cyc} cycles (CPI {cpi}), and stalls reach {wait}%.",
  "ch1.say.miss": "When a {m}-cycle memory miss occurs, instruction I1 (the second instruction) is completely blocked in the ID stage, turning the entire pipeline into a prolonged stall: total execution takes {cyc} cycles, CPI jumps to {cpi}, and {wait}% of cycles are spent idling for memory.",
  "ch1.p3": "Running this 6-instruction loop through the simulator: an L1 hit (1 cycle) with forwarding takes only 11 cycles (9.1% stalls); disabling forwarding immediately pushes it to 18 cycles; and a single main memory miss (200 cycles) sends the total duration jumping to 210 cycles—where 200 cycles (95.2%) of pipeline time are wasted swallowing bubbles.",
  "ch1.limit": "<strong>Model simplifications:</strong> This is an in-order, single-issue 5-stage pipeline (the classic Hennessy–Patterson teaching model). High-performance modern CPUs use out-of-order execution and branch prediction to find hundreds of independent instructions to fill vacant slots, but once the instruction window fills with dependent operations, they still grind to a halt.",

  /* ───── 02 ───── */
  "ch2.title": "Memory Is Far Away: Latency Ladders and Cache Hits",
  "ch2.law": "A computation takes under a nanosecond, but fetching a number from main memory takes dozens of nanoseconds; sequential access consumes an entire cache line completely, while strided jumping leaves every read waiting in vain.",
  "ch2.p1": "Why is memory so much slower? Because <strong>main memory (DRAM)</strong> built from capacitor arrays is dense and cheap, but charging and discharging to read a bit takes tens of nanoseconds, and signals must travel across copper PCB traces and in and out of chip pins. By contrast, <strong>static cache (SRAM)</strong> sitting right next to the processor core uses six transistors per bit and can be read in a nanosecond or two—at the cost of consuming vastly more silicon area and offering orders of magnitude less capacity. Chips are therefore built as a pyramid: closer means smaller and faster; farther means larger and slower.",
  "ch2.p2": "If we scale 1 clock cycle of a ~3.3 GHz processor (~0.3 nanoseconds) up to <strong>1 second</strong> in human time, the physical chasm across levels becomes immediately intuitive:",
  "ch2.lv.title": "Memory Hierarchy Latency Scaled to Human Time",
  "ch2.lv.how": "Toggle between logarithmic and linear scales. On a linear scale, solid-state drives crush all other levels into invisible single pixels, but the practical gap is equally stark across the 80× span between L1 (1 ns) and DRAM (80 ns).",
  "ch2.lv.aria": "Horizontal bar chart comparing access latencies across L1, L2, L3, DRAM main memory, and NVMe SSD",
  "ch2.lv.scale": "Scale",
  "ch2.lv.log": "Logarithmic",
