  "ch2.read.waitbench": "Click button to benchmark",
  "ch2.cv.mat": "16×16 Matrix Access Map",
  "ch2.cv.stats": "Cache Utilization & Traffic",
  "ch2.cv.missrate": "Miss rate {r}%",
  "ch2.say.row": "Row-major reads consume adjacent elements in the same cache line in one go: with a {cap} B cache and {blk} B line size, it misses only {miss} times ({rate}%), transferring {bytes} bytes.",
  "ch2.say.col": "Column-major reads jump an entire row (128 bytes) each step; by the time the next column begins, the newly fetched line has already been evicted: missing {miss} times ({rate}%), transferring {bytes} bytes.",
  "ch2.say.colfit": "Column-major reads jump 128 bytes per step, but this {cap} B cache holds the entire 2,048 B matrix without evictions: only {miss} cold misses ({rate}%), transferring {bytes} bytes.",
  "ch2.p3": "When hardware fetches data from memory, it never fetches just 8 bytes; it fetches an entire <strong>cache line (typically 64 bytes)</strong> at once. In a default 1 KB direct-mapped cache with 64-byte lines: traversing a 16×16 matrix row-wise (256 reads) misses only <strong>32 times</strong> (a 12.5% miss rate, transferring exactly 2,048 bytes). Switching to column-major traversal with the exact same number of additions causes misses to explode to <strong>256 times</strong> (a 100% miss rate, transferring 16,384 bytes—a massive <strong>8×</strong> increase in data movement). (With larger matrices or strides that match a multiple of a 1-way cache's capacity, conflict misses evict lines even before capacity is full—higher associativity exists specifically to prevent that.)",
  "ch2.limit": "<strong>Benchmarking notes and simplifications:</strong> Real CPUs feature hardware stride prefetchers and JavaScript engines perform array bounds checking, so the column/row time ratio measured in browsers will fluctuate across devices (the benchmark takes the median of 7 runs); but the physical reality that spatial locality governs total data movement remains unchanged.",

  /* ───── 03 ───── */
  "ch3.title": "Locality and Tiling: The Same Algorithm, Ten Times the Traffic",
  "ch3.law": "Two pieces of code with the exact same O(N³) multiplications in an algorithms textbook can differ in execution speed by several-fold or even tenfold—not because the arithmetic count differs, but because of whether each number is reused enough once loaded into cache.",
  "ch3.p1": "Big-O notation counts only arithmetic operations, pretending every memory access takes identical time. Multiplying two N×N matrices C = A × B takes a fixed 2N³ floating-point operations. But in a naive <code>i-j-k</code> triple loop, the innermost loop strides down matrix B by column, evicting freshly cached lines on almost every read of B.",
  "ch3.p2": "Since cache cannot hold entire matrices, the standard engineering solution is <strong>tiling (or blocking)</strong>: carve the matrices into small B×B tiles and compute one tile at a time, keeping all three tiles of A, B, and C (3B² numbers total) resident in fast cache for extensive reuse before moving to the next tile.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Matrix Multiplication Cache Tiling Simulator (24×24, 2 KB 4-Way Cache)",
  "ch3.model.how": "Switch loop orders (naive i-j-k, row-friendly i-k-j, and tiled), then drag tile size B. Green bars indicate that the 3 sub-blocks fit within 2 KB cache; red indicates spill. Notice the difference between optimal tiling B = 8 and cache thrashing when B = 12 spills.",
  "ch3.model.aria": "Bar chart comparing cache misses between i-j-k, i-k-j, and different tiled block sizes B",
  "ch3.leg.fits": "Sub-blocks fit in 2 KB cache",
  "ch3.leg.spill": "Working set spills 2 KB cache",
