  "ch3.ctl.b": "Tile size B",
  "ch3.read.flops": "Total FLOPs (fixed)",
  "ch3.read.miss": "Cache misses",
  "ch3.read.bytes": "DRAM traffic",
  "ch3.read.ai": "Measured arithmetic intensity",
  "ch3.cv.x": "Implementation / Tile size",
  "ch3.cv.y": "Cache misses",
  "ch3.say.ijk": "Naive i-j-k performs the same {flops} operations, but triggers {miss} cache misses, moving {bytes} bytes (only {ai} operations per byte moved).",
  "ch3.say.ikj": "Simply reordering the inner loop to i-k-j so B is read row-wise cuts misses to {miss} ({ai} FLOP/byte), eliminating more than half of the data traffic.",
  "ch3.say.fit": "With tile size B = {b}, all three sub-blocks require only {ws} B, fitting snugly inside the 2 KB cache: misses plummet to {miss} ({bytes} B moved), raising effective arithmetic intensity to {ai} FLOP/byte.",
  "ch3.say.spill": "Tile size B = {b} is too large! The three sub-blocks together need {ws} B, exceeding the 2 KB cache capacity, causing lines to be repeatedly evicted before computing finishes: misses rebound to {miss}.",
  "ch3.p3": "Simulating a 24×24 double-precision matrix multiplication in a real cache (fixed 27,648 FLOPs, 2 KB 4-way set-associative cache, 64-byte lines): naive <code>i-j-k</code> incurs <strong>4,035 misses</strong> (transferring 258,240 bytes, only 0.11 FLOPs per byte); swapping the loop order to <code>i-k-j</code> drops misses to <strong>1,872</strong>; and applying <strong>B = 8 tiling</strong>—aligned to 64-byte cache lines (8 doubles) with 3×8²×8 = 1,536 B ≤ 2,048 B—slashes misses to just <strong>534</strong> (transferring only 34,176 bytes, 0.81 FLOP/byte), an immediate <strong>7.6× reduction</strong> in memory traffic. Enlarging B to 12 (3,456 B, spilling cache) immediately pushes misses back up to 1,145.",
  "ch3.limit": "<strong>Model simplifications:</strong> Real high-performance matrix libraries (such as BLAS or cuBLAS) employ multi-level hierarchical tiling (register micro-kernels + L1 + L2 + L3/shared memory) and pack tiles into contiguous memory to avoid associativity conflicts, following the exact same principles demonstrated by B = 8 here.",

  /* ───── 04 ───── */
  "ch4.title": "The Memory Wall and Little's Law: Latency, Bandwidth, and Concurrency",
  "ch4.law": "Even with a 98% cache hit rate, the remaining 2% of misses multiplied by hundreds of cycles of DRAM latency will still wipe out over half your compute; and to truly saturate a high-speed bus, the system must keep thousands of requests in flight at once.",
  "ch4.p1": "In their famous 1995 paper introducing the <strong>Memory Wall</strong>, Wulf and McKee ran a starkly simple calculation: in the standard average memory access time formula, if even a tiny fraction of requests pierce the cache and reach main memory, the prolonged latency acts like an anchor dragging down the whole machine:",
  "ch4.eq": "Average Memory Access Time AMAT = t<sub>hit</sub> + miss rate m × t<sub>miss</sub> &nbsp;|&nbsp; In-flight data = Bandwidth × Latency (Little's Law)",
  "ch4.p2": "Beyond waiting for a single request (<strong>latency</strong>, in nanoseconds), there is another wall: <strong>bandwidth</strong> (bytes per second, in GB/s). According to Little's Law from queueing theory, an HBM3 bus with an 80 ns round-trip latency and 3,350 GB/s peak bandwidth requires <strong>3,350 GB/s × 80 ns = 268 KB</strong> of outstanding data in flight to remain fully saturated. At 128 bytes per transaction, that requires issuing approximately <strong>2,094</strong> mutually independent memory requests simultaneously.",
  "ch4.model.title": "The Memory Wall (AMAT) and Little's Law Concurrency Slots",
  "ch4.model.how": "Drag the cache miss rate to see compute utilization collapse, then adjust memory latency and the number of in-flight requests to see when achieved bandwidth reaches the 3,350 GB/s peak.",
  "ch4.model.aria": "Left bar chart shows compute cycles swallowed by memory stalls; right curve shows in-flight requests versus achieved bandwidth",
  "ch4.leg.bw": "Little's Law bandwidth curve",
  "ch4.leg.knee": "Concurrency required for peak bandwidth",
  "ch4.ctl.miss": "Cache miss rate m",
  "ch4.ctl.lat": "Round-trip DRAM latency",
  "ch4.ctl.req": "In-flight requests (128 B each)",
